 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Active Learning Scene Sampling](15489-active-learning-scene-sampling.md)
-Finds optimal camera poses (position and rotation) that minimize the deviation of median render time from a target time budget using Bayesian optimization.
+Active Learning Scene Sampling finds optimal camera poses (position and rotation) that minimize the deviation of median render time from a target time budget using Bayesian optimization. It differs from its parent, Random Camera Sampling, by intelligently selecting viewpoints based on performance feedback rather than uniform random selection.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot K)$ or $O(K)$ depending on implementation, where $N$ is the stack size and $K$ is `num_cams`. The `pop` operation on a list is $O(N)$ in the worst case (shifting elements), making the total complexity $O(K \cdot N)$. If a linked list or swap-and-pop technique were used, it could be $O(K)$. The source does not specify the underlying data structure's pop complexity [S1, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** $O(K)$ for the output list. The input stack is modified in-place, so no additional memory for the stack is required beyond the output.
-**Strengths:** Simple to implement; ensures no duplicate cameras are selected (sampling without replacement); fast for small `num_cams`.
-**Limitations:** Does not guarantee spatial diversity (randomly selected cameras might cluster in one region); performance degrades if `num_cams` is large relative to stack size due to list shifting.
-**When to use:** When a quick, unbiased subset of existing viewpoints is needed for training or rendering, and spatial distribution is not critical.
-**When not to use:** When spatial coverage is important (use [Random Pose Selection](8187-random-pose-selection.md) or active learning); when the stack is very large and performance is critical (consider swap-and-pop or reservoir sampling).
+**Time Complexity:** $O(K)$, where $K$ is `num_cams`. Each iteration involves a random number generation and a list pop operation. In Python, `list.pop(index)` is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the current length of the list, making the total complexity $O(K \cdot N)$ in the worst case if the list is large and indices are near the beginning [G].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(K)$ for storing the output list `camlist`. The input stack is modified in place.
+**Strengths:** Simple to implement; guarantees no duplicate cameras in the sample; ensures uniform probability for each remaining camera at each step.
+**Limitations:** Modifies the input data structure; performance degrades if the stack is very large and many samples are drawn due to $O(N)$ pop operations.
 
